This document illustrates and describes the Markdown syntax supported by this boardgamegeek.com (BGG) markup rendering program.

The basic syntax follows the [url=https://spec.commonmark.org/0.29]CommonMark markdown[/url] syntax.

[size=24]Basic Markdown[/size]

Headers: only level 1 (#) and level 2 (##) headers are supported.

Emphasis: this is [i]important[/i] (rendered as italics).

Strong emphasis: this is [b]very important[/b] (rendered as bold).

Lists are not supported on BGG, and are left as is:

* First item
* Second item

Numbered list are not supported on BGG either. They are rendered as a continuous sequence of numbers, starting with the first number. Thus,

1. First item
2. Second item

will automatically produce:

1. First item
2. Second item

Quotes follow the standard Markdown syntax:

[q]This is an example where the source lines are automatically concatenated in the output.

Any line that ends like this one with two spaces
will not be concatenated with the following one, as is normal in Markdown.
[/q]
[url=https://google.com]External links[/url] follow the usual Markdown syntax.

[size=24]Paragraphs[/size]

In Markdown, paragraphs can be written on many lines, an generally result in a single rendered paragraph (typically as if line breaks were replaced by spaces). This renderer follows the same convention. Thus, this paragraph, which is written on multiple lines, will be rendered as a single paragraph on BGG (with line breaks that appear on screen at generally different positions.

As in Markdown, if a line ends with 2 blank spaces, then a line break at its end is kept in the rendered output:

This line will remain by itself as it ends with two blank spaces.
Second line of the same paragraph, that doesn't end with blank spaces. This line will be concatenated with the previous one.

[size=24]Internal BGG links[/size]

[size=18]Full URL form[/size]

Internal links can be entered as standard Markdown links:

This is a [article=36994502][b]great[/b] answer[/article].

Names (like the name of a board game) are inserted automatically, if omitted:

This game is similar to [boardgame=224517][/boardgame].

[size=24]Images and videos[/size]

Images and videos can be inserted with a syntax similar to that of links (with a full URL).

Images use a syntax similar to that of CommonMark markdown, except that the alternate text part ("[text]") is simply absent (like when inserting a link to a boardgame without specifying the name of the boardgame). In fact, BGG doesn't provider alternate text for images.

[size=18]Full URL form[/size]

Internal BGG images can be followed by their [url=https://boardgamegeek.com/wiki/page/Wiki_Image_Sizes#]BGG size[/url].

Internal BGG image:

Default size: [imageid=2355823]
Large size: [imageid=2355823 large]

Technical note: the exclamation mark is there to explicitly indicate that a link points to an image, so that users can also write regular URLs inside parentheses (which are rendered as is). Example:

You can also do a direct search (https://www.google.com).

(If there had been a "!" prefix, this would mean that the address pointed to an image to be directly rendered in the BGG page, instead.)

